<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthage</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carthaginian, Carthaginians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthage was a Phoenician city founded on the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, traditionally in the ninth century BCE, which became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>. It dominated trade in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and fought three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which destroyed it in 146 BCE. Its trade networks reached into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, including contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthage.jpg" alt="Carthage" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ELEL09, montage · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montage_ville_de_Carthage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Carthage was founded on the eastern side of the Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Tradition credits Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Elissa</a>, or Dido, originally from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, though her historicity has been questioned. In the myth she asked a local tribe for land and was told she could have as much as an oxhide could cover, so she cut the hide into strips and laid out the perimeter of the city. The name derives from the Punic qrt-ḥdšt, new city, implying a new Tyre. The Carthaginians were Phoenician settlers who spoke Canaanite, a Semitic language, and followed a local variety of Canaanite religion. Unlike other colonisers who required only respect from their colonies, Carthage is said to have sent its own magistrates to govern overseas settlements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city stood on a promontory between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the Tunisian coast, so that every ship crossing the sea passed it. It had two artificial harbours, one for a navy of 220 warships and one for trade, and walls described as 37 kilometres long, with the 4.0 to 4.8 kilometres across the isthmus truly massive and never penetrated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byrsa">Byrsa</a> citadel stood roughly in the middle. The surrounding farmland, the Chora, was worked by Punic owners, and a retired general named Mago wrote a 28-volume work on agriculture in Punic, later translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek and now lost except for passages in other Latin works.</p><p>Carthage contested the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with Greek cities in the Sicilian Wars and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhic-war">Pyrrhic War</a>, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> destroyed the city in 146 BCE after a siege of nearly three years, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byrsa">Byrsa</a> being the last place to fall.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a> founded a short-lived colony there by 122 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> built a new city between 49 and 44 BCE. By the first century it was the second-largest city in the western half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, with a peak population of 500,000, and the centre of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 698 Hasan ibn al-Nu'man defeated Emperor Tiberios III in the Battle of Carthage, and the Umayyad forces destroyed Roman Carthage, tearing down its walls and cutting its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> to prevent a Byzantine return. In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a>, massacring its inhabitants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a>, and the Hafsids then destroyed its defences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Christian Tuxen Falbe surveyed the site in 1830, and Charles Ernest Beulé, Alfred Louis Delattre and French archaeologists after 1921 excavated it. Scholars disagree on whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a> was practised in ancient Carthage. Carthage became a municipality in 1919 as a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, and the ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carthage” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Acts of the Scillitan Martyrs, about Christians tried at <b>Carthage</b> in 180, are the oldest dated Christian document written in Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Celtiberians served widely as mercenaries: in 211 BCE some 20,000 hired by Rome deserted Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio, contributing to the destruction of both Scipio brothers' armies, and about 4,000 fought for <b>Carthage</b> at the Battle of the Great Plains in 203 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Competition for sea routes brought the cities into alliance with <b>Carthage</b> against the Phocaean Greeks, who in the 6th century BCE were founding colonies on the coasts of Sardinia, Spain and Corsica.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty’s greatest test came in 1270, when Louis IX of France landed at <b>Carthage</b> on what became the Eighth Crusade; disease ravaged the camp, Louis died near Tunis in August, and Charles of Anjou negotiated a treaty in which al-Mustansir paid an indemnity and renewed tribute to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late third century BCE the lands west of <b>Carthage</b> were held by two Berber peoples, the Massylii in the east and the larger Masaesyli in the west, whose king Syphax ruled from Siga and later from Cirta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After destroying <b>Carthage</b>, the Roman Senate ordered the farming treatise of the Carthaginian writer Mago, in twenty-eight books, to be translated into Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
